Frame by Frame visualizer

[] Find a way to upload all the animation data per model where is easy to know which object is drawing and access the corresponding index for position.

[] Be able to draw a mesh and manually show the different frames (maybe by a key press)

[] Have multiple objects with multiple different animations and be able to select each object and play each frame of each animation of that object.

Technical stuff:

We need 2 Graphics Pipelines one for static mesh and one for animated mesh.

the current vertex shader is

```
layout(location = 0) in vec3 inPosition;
layout(location = 1) in vec3 inColor;

layout(location = 0) out vec3 fragColor;

layout(binding = 0) uniform CameraBufferObject {
    mat4 view;
    mat4 proj;
} camera;
```

That's the static vertex.

For the animated one we can do something like:

```
layout(location = 1) in vec3 inColor;

layout(binding = 0) uniform CameraBufferObject {
    mat4 view;
    mat4 proj;
} camera;
layout(std430, set = 0, binding = 1) readonly buffer Models {
    GpuAnimationModel models[];
};
layout(std430, set = 0, binding = 2) readonly buffer Clips {
    GpuAnimationClip clips[];
};
layout(std430, set = 0, binding = 3) readonly buffer Frames {
    GpuAnimationFrame frames[];
};
layout(std430, set = 0, binding = 4) readonly buffer Positions {
    vec4 positions[];
};

layout(location = 0) out vec3 fragColor;

GpuAnimationModel model = models[modelIndex];
GpuAnimationClip clip = clips[model.clipOffset + clipIndex];
GpuAnimationFrame frame = frames[clip.frameOffset + frameIndex];

vec3 animatedPosition =
    positions[frame.positionOffset + vertexIndex].xyz;
```

So Vertex for this animated shader should only have the inColor. It can be called "AnimatedVertex"

